{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791129992,"nonce":"ySHPTWWHs07JU2t0NRPwlaMJnFQ=","title":"caribou_abi: a borrowed string parameter, so string-taking plugin calls bind directly","message":"A plugin's string parameter is `Text`: one word, the address of a core string (a header and its bytes). A language that calls a plugin directly (Zyntax's native bindings, zyntax git-bug 578bf3e) holds its strings in its own layout and can pass a borrowed (ptr, len) at no cost. Turning that into a core string means an allocation per call. So in the first cut of direct bindings, a plugin method with a string parameter stays on the protocol path.\n\nAdd a borrowed string parameter kind to caribou_abi, for example `Str` / `\u0026str` with its own TypeTag, passed as two words (pointer, byte length):\n- The word thunk takes two words for it.\n- Ash, Wren and the protocol path pass a core string's bytes and length, borrowed for the call.\n- A direct binding passes the caller's own bytes.\n\nCheck the C ABI on each target. A 16-byte struct by value is two registers on SysV x86-64 and AAPCS64 but goes by reference on Windows x64, so the thunk and the direct binding should take two scalar parameters, not a struct.","files":null}]}